kg:note_ref24608_scopecontent_0_p_0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdf:value "- Finding aid title reads, \"Workmen's payroll, Samarra January 9, 1911 - August 30, 1911.\" [Joseph M. Upton's Catalogue of the Herzfeld Archive, compiled in 1974]" .

kg:note_ref24608_scopecontent_1_p_0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdf:value "- Additional information reads, \"Only the payroll booklet of the first campaign is preserved in the Sackler Archives. From this material it becomes clear that normally one crew of workers consisted of 20-25 men led by a foreman. For most of the time Herzfeld tried to maintain seven crews. After he had lost at least 50% of his workers in late spring and summer 1911, his efforts to make up the losses can be seen from the payroll of August 30, 1911. His workforce had expanded to 9 crews with most of them having 27-29 men. From a note in one of the letters to Sarre we learn that the normal working day lasted 10 hours. Only during Ramadan was the time reduced to 9 hours; see AP IV, Letter of August 5. 1911.\" [Leisten, Thomas, 2003: \"Excavation of Samarra, v. I. Architecture : Final report of the first campaign 1910-1912. Verlag Philipp von Zabern, Mainz am Rhein, 2003. p. 15.\"]" .

